    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The objective of this project is to use computational modeling to improve understanding of hydraulic stimulation in Enhanced Geothermal Systems and identify techniques to improve economic performance. This paper summarizes current progress and results.\r\n\r\nHydraulic stimulation in Enhanced Geothermal Systems is performed by injecting water at high pressure into low permeability, typically crystalline rock. In most cases, the elevated pore pressure induces slip on preexisting fractures, resulting in an increase in permeability. This process involves interacting hydraulic, thermal, mechanical, and thermoelastic processes. An important factor is the geometry of the preexisting fracture network.\r\n\r\nA simulator was written that performs flow simulation on an unstructured discrete fracture network and uses a boundary element code to calculate how the injection induces slip and opens fractures. The code can simulate complex networks containing hundreds of fractures. Most studies in the EGS modeling literature neglect that when fractures slip, they perturb the stress around them. Our work includes stress perturbation effects and shows that stress perturbation has a profound impact on the resulting fracture network.\r\n\r\nSlip events can be correlated to microseismicity. Because we assume quasi-static deformation and use a simplified treatment of friction, our model is not a true earthquake model. However, it mimics the process of seismicity, and should be able to qualitatively cast light on how fluid pressure distribution could affect seismicity during injection into a fracture. Different injection strategies were modeled in a single isolated fracture. Strategies were identified that might reduce microseismicity during stimulation.\r\n\r\nThere are five objectives for future work: (1) refine the existing modeling code by making improvements such as extending to three dimensions, (2) develop training images of subsurface fracture networks and develop stochastic methods of generating realizations that resemble these training images (3) through modeling, test a variety of stimulation strategies to identify strategies that minimize induced seismicity and maximize economic performance, (4) perform sensitivity analysis to identify the most important physical processes and biggest uncertainties in EGS stimulation, and (5) perform in depth study of specific physical mechanisms that may important, such as the formation of splays during slip.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
